Include actor, action, target, timestamp, outcome, and relevant contextual metadata. Never log passwords, secrets, session tokens, or unnecessary private content.

Protect audit logs against unauthorized alteration and deletion.

## 6. GRANULAR ROLE AND PERMISSION ENGINE

Implement centralized permissions rather than scattered role-name checks throughout the application.

Use capabilities such as:

- ads.create
- ads.read.own
- ads.read.all
- ads.update.own
- ads.update.all
- ads.publish
- ads.moderate
- ads.delete.own
- ads.delete.all
- users.read
- users.suspend
- users.manage_roles
- categories.manage
- reports.manage
- promotions.manage
- analytics.read
- settings.manage
- audit.read
- permissions.manage

Adapt the exact permission names to the current architecture.

Implement:

- A centralized authorization service.
- Explicit permission checks for backend operations.
- Ownership checks for private resources.
- Least-privilege defaults.
- Deny-by-default behavior.
- Server-side validation.
- Protected API endpoints.
- Permission-aware navigation and actions.
- Audit logging for privileged operations.
- Tests for permitted and denied actions.

A higher role may receive additional capabilities, but role hierarchy alone must not automatically grant access to every resource.

Prevent privilege escalation, insecure direct object references, unauthorized role changes, and cross-user data access.

Where appropriate, require two-person approval for especially sensitive operations such as granting the highest privilege or performing irreversible bulk actions.

